The word is at most 100 characters, so checking every start position and extending to the right is fast enough.

For each start index `i`, extend `j` rightward while `word[j]` is a vowel. Keep a set of the vowels seen so far. Whenever the set reaches size 5, count 1, because the substring `word[i..j]` is a valid vowel substring. Stop extending as soon as a consonant appears. Each valid `(i, j)` pair is counted exactly once.

The input examples contain trailing spaces (e.g. `"aeiouu "`), which are probably formatting artifacts. If a space were really present, it is a non-vowel and simply ends the extension, so the logic still works.

```python
class Solution:
    def countVowelSubstrings(self, word: str) -> int:
        vowels = set("aeiou")
        n = len(word)
        count = 0
        for i in range(n):
            seen = set()
            for j in range(i, n):
                c = word[j]
                if c not in vowels:
                    break
                seen.add(c)
                if len(seen) == 5:
                    count += 1
        return count
```

